\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {30}{\ignorespaces Impulse responses to a one-standard-deviation arrivals shock, new data panel, 2007M5--2025M6 ($n=218$, $p=3$ lags), including a COVID dummy (2020M3--2021M10). Bayesian VAR with a diffuse prior and Cholesky identification (variable ordering: arrivals, business expectations, consumer confidence, industrial production, unemployment, government immigration sentiment). Lines show the posterior median; shaded areas show 68\% and 90\% credible bands.}}{52}{figure.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {31}{\ignorespaces Impulse responses to a one-standard-deviation arrivals shock, new data panel, 2007M5--2025M6 ($n=218$, $p=2$ lags), including a COVID dummy (2020M3--2021M10). Bayesian VAR with a diffuse prior and Cholesky identification (variable ordering: arrivals, business expectations, consumer confidence, industrial production, unemployment, opposition immigration sentiment). Lines show the posterior median; shaded areas show 68\% and 90\% credible bands.}}{53}{figure.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {32}{\ignorespaces Forecast error variance decomposition for the model with opposition immigration sentiment, new data panel, 2007M5--2025M6 ($n=218$, $p=2$ lags), including a COVID dummy (2020M3--2021M10). Bars show the posterior median share of each variable's forecast error variance attributable to the unemployment shock (blue) and to all other shocks (orange). Bayesian VAR with a diffuse prior and Cholesky identification (variable ordering: arrivals, business expectations, consumer confidence, industrial production, unemployment, opposition immigration sentiment); horizon in months.}}{54}{figure.32}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {33}{\ignorespaces Forecast error variance decomposition for the model with sentiment scored by GPT-4.1-nano, new data panel, 2007M5--2025M6 ($n=218$, $p=2$ lags), including a COVID dummy (2020M3--2021M10). Bars show the posterior median share of each variable's forecast error variance attributable to the opposition immigration sentiment shock (blue) and to all other shocks (orange). Bayesian VAR with a diffuse prior and Cholesky identification (sentiment ordered last); horizon in months.}}{55}{figure.33}%
